.th HT IV 8/76
.sh NAME
ht \*- RH-11/TU-16 magtape interface
.sh DESCRIPTION
The special files
.it "/dev/mt0, /dev/ht0,"
etc. refer to the TU-16 magtape.  Raw and cooked interfaces with
various options are provided as follows:
.s3
.lp +7 4
1.  An
.it r
prefix indicates the raw device; no
.it r
means cooked.  See below.
.lp +7 4
2.  An
.it n
prefix indicates a no-rewind device.  This facility allows
multi-file tapes to be read and written.  See below.
.lp +7 4
3.  An
.it mt
means 800 BPI; an
.it ht
means 1600 BPI.
.lp +7 4
4.  The digit is the slave select, currently limited to the range
0 to 3 by the driver.
.s3
.i0
Thus,
.it /dev/mt0
is cooked, normal-rewind, 800 BPI slave 0, and
.it rnht0
is raw, no-rewind, 1600 BPI slave 0.  On our system the only slave is 0.
.s3
In the discussion below, the word
.it device
will be used to refer to the special file in question.
.s3
When opened for reading or writing, the tape is assumed to be positioned as 
desired.  When a device is closed, a double tape mark
(alias double filemark) is written
if the device was open for writing.  Then if the device in question is
normal-rewind, the tape is rewound.  If it is no-rewind and the device
was open for writing, the tape is positioned before the second tape
mark just written.  Finally, if the device is no-rewind but was not
open for writing, the tape is positioned after the next tape mark
following the data just read.
What all this means is that
.sp 1
.ce 3
cat /dev/null >/dev/mt0
dd if=f1 of=/dev/nmt0  
dd if=f2 of=/dev/mt0   
.sp 1
rewinds the tape, then writes f1, a tape mark, f2, and a double tape
mark, and leaves the tape rewound, and that
.sp 1
.ce 2
cat /dev/rnmt0 >g1     
cat /dev/mt0 >g2       
.s3
immediately thereafter makes g1 and g2 copies of f1 and f2 (almost)
and leaves the tape rewound.
.s3
A standard tape consists of a series of tape files ("files" in the
magtape sense, not the Unix sense) separated by tape marks and 
ended by a pair of tape marks.  A cooked tape special file allows
one to treat portions of such a tape much like any other file.  Seeks and variable-sized
or intermixed reads and writes work.  Writing, however, must not involve skipping
beyond the current end-of-tape, and any write truncates the tape
beyond where the write occurred.  A tape mark
is returned as an error from a cooked read,
and the tape is left positioned before the
tape mark so that no-rewind devices work as expected
when a close is done.  Such devices
can be used to position to a specific file on the tape by opening for
read and closing the device a number of times.  Note that once opened,
a cooked device will only permit reading between the position when
opened and the next tape mark.
.s3
The raw interface must be used to handle non-standard tapes.  Each
.it read
or
.it write
call reads or writes the next tape record.  Written records are always
the length requested (remember that there is a minimum length
for NRZI records).
A read normally returns an entire tape record; however, records
longer than the request length
are truncated without fanfare and the extra bytes discarded,
so take care!  The buffer should consist of an integral number of words;
both base and length must be even.  A tape mark is returned as a zero-length
read, i.e., an end-of-file.  The tape is left positioned beyond it, so
the next read will return the first record of the next file.
.sp 1
.ce
cat /dev/rnht0 >junk
.sp 1
copies up to the next tape mark and leaves the tape positioned
after it.
.sh FILES
/dev/mt?, /dev/nmt?, /dev/rmt?, /dev/rnmt?,
.br
/dev/ht?, /dev/nht?, /dev/rht?, /dev/rnht?
.sh "SEE ALSO"
tp(I)
.sh BUGS
Raw I/O should provide positioning and tape-mark-writing functions.
EOT is not handled in any reasonable or predictable way.
Varied and useful error codes should be returned.
.s3
The driver only handles one TM02
and probably has buggy multi-slave handling.
However, no one seems
to have more than one transport, much less two TM02's.
.sh AUTHOR
Larry Wehr, Piscataway Labs.
